  <h2 id='server'>14.2 The server data layer</h2>
  <p>
    The pattern that makes all of Part C work:
    <strong
      >every server data access lives in one module, and every function in it is wrapped in
      <code>cache()</code></strong
    >. <Chip k='next' />
    <Chip k='critical' /> Components never call
    <code>fetch</code> or the database directly: they call the data layer, from anywhere in the tree,
    as many times as composition requires.
  </p>

  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-good'
      is:raw><span class="filename">data/programs.ts</span><span class="tok-kw">import</span> { cache } <span class="tok-kw">from</span> <span class="tok-str">"react"</span>;
<span class="tok-com">// This import turns any leak into a client bundle into a build error.</span>
<span class="tok-kw">import</span> <span class="tok-str">"server-only"</span>;

<span class="tok-com">/* This is a per-request memo: any component may call it with the same id,
   and it executes once per request, no matter who asks or how often. */</span>
<span class="tok-kw">export const</span> <span class="tok-fn">getProgram</span> = <span class="tok-fn">cache</span>(<span class="tok-kw">async</span> (id: <span class="tok-fn">string</span>) =&gt; {
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> res = <span class="tok-kw">await</span> <span class="tok-fn">fetch</span>(<span class="tok-str">`${API_URL}/programs/${id}`</span>, { headers: <span class="tok-kw">await</span> <span class="tok-fn">authHeaders</span>() });
  <span class="tok-kw">if</span> (!res.ok) <span class="tok-kw">throw new</span> <span class="tok-fn">ProgramError</span>(res);
  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> res.json() <span class="tok-kw">as</span> <span class="tok-fn">Promise</span>&lt;<span class="tok-fn">Program</span>&gt;;
});</pre>
  </Snippet>

  <p>
    Two subtleties worth knowing. First, <code>fetch</code> itself dedupes identical calls within a render
    pass <Chip k='critical' />, but
    <code>cache()</code> is the habit to build, because it also covers database clients, SDKs, and computed
    values, and it gives the function a single authoritative home. Second, <code>cache()</code>'s
    lifetime is <em>one request</em>: it is a dedupe mechanism, not a performance cache. User A's
    request never sees user B's data through it, which is exactly what you want for anything
    personalized.
  </p>
  <p>
    When data is <em>not</em> personalized and survives across requests (templates, org lists, filter
    options, reference data), promote it one layer up with
    <code>"use cache"</code>: <Chip k='next' />
    <Chip k='very' />
  </p>

  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-good'
      is:raw><span class="filename">data/templates.ts</span><span class="tok-kw">import</span> { cacheLife, cacheTag } <span class="tok-kw">from</span> <span class="tok-str">"next/cache"</span>;

<span class="tok-kw">export async function</span> <span class="tok-fn">getFormTemplates</span>() {
  <span class="tok-com">// The output is cached across requests AND users.</span>
  <span class="tok-dir">"use cache"</span>;
  <span class="tok-com">// The acceptable staleness is declared, not implied.</span>
  <span class="tok-fn">cacheLife</span>(<span class="tok-str">"hours"</span>);
  <span class="tok-com">// The tag is a handle for targeted invalidation.</span>
  <span class="tok-fn">cacheTag</span>(<span class="tok-str">"form-templates"</span>);
  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> api.templates.<span class="tok-fn">list</span>();
}</pre>
  </Snippet>
  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-good'
      is:raw><span class="filename">actions/templates.ts</span><span class="tok-dir">"use server"</span>;
<span class="tok-kw">import</span> { revalidateTag } <span class="tok-kw">from</span> <span class="tok-str">"next/cache"</span>;

<span class="tok-kw">export async function</span> <span class="tok-fn">createTemplate</span>(input: <span class="tok-fn">TemplateInput</span>) {
  <span class="tok-kw">await</span> api.templates.<span class="tok-fn">create</span>(input);
  <span class="tok-com">// Every reader updates: no callbacks up the tree, no refetchAll().</span>
  <span class="tok-fn">revalidateTag</span>(<span class="tok-str">"form-templates"</span>);
}</pre>
  </Snippet>
  <p>
    Note what the mutation does <em>not</em> do: it doesn't call a callback up to some parent, doesn't
    set a consistency token in the URL, doesn't orchestrate a
    <code>refetchAll()</code>. It invalidates the tag, and every component that reads
    <code>getFormTemplates()</code>, wherever it sits in the tree, gets fresh data on the next
    render. That's <a href='13-minimize-the-wires.html#callbacks'>§13.4</a>'s "communicate through
    the cache" made concrete.
  </p>

  <h2 id='client'>14.3 The client data layer</h2>
  <p>
    Some data genuinely must load from the client: it only exists after an interaction, or it polls,
    or it's driven by rapidly-changing client state. For that:
    <strong
      >one shared hook per question, built on SWR (or React Query), keyed canonically. Never raw <code
        >fetch</code
      > in a component, never fetch-in-effect.</strong
    >
    <Chip k='react' />
    <Chip k='critical' />
  </p>

  <Compare>
    <CompareCol kind='bad' label='✕ Hand-rolled: no dedupe layer exists'>
      <Snippet>
        <pre
          class='code v-bad'
          is:raw><span class="filename">every component, separately</span><span class="tok-dir">"use client"</span>;
<span class="tok-kw">const</span> [program, setProgram] = <span class="tok-fn">useState</span>(<span class="tok-kw">null</span>);
<span class="tok-kw">const</span> [isLoading, setIsLoading] = <span class="tok-fn">useState</span>(<span class="tok-kw">true</span>);
<span class="tok-fn">useEffect</span>(() =&gt; {
  <span class="tok-fn">fetch</span>(<span class="tok-str">`/api/proxy/programs/${id}`</span>)
    .<span class="tok-fn">then</span>(r =&gt; r.<span class="tok-fn">json</span>())
    .<span class="tok-fn">then</span>(setProgram)
    .<span class="tok-fn">finally</span>(() =&gt; <span class="tok-fn">setIsLoading</span>(<span class="tok-kw">false</span>));
}, [id]);</pre>
        <CodeCaption>
          Two components asking this way means two requests, two loading states, and two error
          paths, with zero sharing between them.
        </CodeCaption>
      </Snippet>
    </CompareCol>
    <CompareCol kind='good' label='✓ Shared hook: the key IS the dedupe'>
      <Snippet>
        <pre
          class='code v-good'
          is:raw><span class="filename">hooks/useProgram.ts</span><span class="tok-dir">"use client"</span>;
<span class="tok-kw">import</span> useSWR <span class="tok-kw">from</span> <span class="tok-str">"swr"</span>;

<span class="tok-kw">export function</span> <span class="tok-fn">useProgram</span>(id: <span class="tok-fn">string</span>) {
  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> <span class="tok-fn">useSWR</span>&lt;<span class="tok-fn">Program</span>&gt;(<span class="tok-str">`programs/${id}`</span>, fetcher);
}</pre>
        <CodeCaption>
          Any number of components can call this hook with the same id: one cache entry, one
          request, revalidated on focus and updated by <code>mutate()</code>.
        </CodeCaption>
      </Snippet>
    </CompareCol>
  </Compare>

  <Callout kind='warn' title='Client cache is a complement, not the architecture'>
    <p>
      SWR's cache lives in browser memory: it evaporates on a hard navigation and starts empty for
      every visitor. It cannot make first render fast; only the server layers can (that's Part A).
      The division of labor:
      <strong
        >server cache for what the page is born with; client cache for what it learns afterwards.</strong
      >
      If you find most of a page's data flowing through SWR, the problem isn't the caching: the fetching
      is on the wrong side of the boundary.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id='choosing'>14.4 Choosing the layer</h2>
  <table class='tbl'>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>The data is…</th>
        <th>Use</th>
        <th>Example</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Needed at first render, personalized</strong></td>
        <td><code>cache()</code> in the server data layer</td>
        <td>program detail, user profile, permissions</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Needed at first render, shared across users</strong></td>
        <td><code>"use cache"</code> + <code>cacheLife</code>/<code>cacheTag</code></td>
        <td>templates, org lists, filter options, reference data</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Born from an interaction</strong></td>
        <td>SWR / React Query shared hook</td>
        <td>search-as-you-type results, autocomplete</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Live / polling</strong></td>
        <td>SWR with <code>refreshInterval</code></td>
        <td>job status, notifications badge</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Changed by a mutation</strong></td>
        <td>
          Action + <code>revalidateTag</code> (server) / <code>mutate()</code> (client)
        </td>
        <td>everything above, after a write</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2 id='extends-fetch'>14.5 How Next.js extends fetch</h2>
  <p>
    One thing that trips people up: <code>fetch</code> on the server does not behave like
    <code>fetch</code> in the browser. Next.js replaces the global <code>fetch</code> with an extended
    version that adds two of the layers above directly to the call.
    <Chip k='next' />
    <Chip k='critical' /> First, it
    <strong>deduplicates identical requests within a single render</strong>: two components that <code
      >fetch</code
    > the same URL with the same options during one pass share one request. Second, it wires the call
    into the <strong>Data Cache</strong>, a persistent, cross-request store you control through the <code
      >cache</code
    > and <code>next</code>
    options.
  </p>

  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-good'
      is:raw><span class="filename">data/stats.ts</span><span class="tok-kw">export async function</span> <span class="tok-fn">getPublicStats</span>() {
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> res = <span class="tok-kw">await</span> <span class="tok-fn">fetch</span>(<span class="tok-str">`${API_URL}/stats`</span>, {
    <span class="tok-com">// Cache the result and treat it as fresh for an hour.</span>
    next: { revalidate: 3600, tags: [<span class="tok-str">"stats"</span>] },
  });
  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> res.json();
}

<span class="tok-com">// Personalized or always-fresh reads opt out explicitly.</span>
<span class="tok-kw">export async function</span> <span class="tok-fn">getLiveQueue</span>() {
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> res = <span class="tok-kw">await</span> <span class="tok-fn">fetch</span>(<span class="tok-str">`${API_URL}/queue`</span>, { cache: <span class="tok-str">"no-store"</span> });
  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> res.json();
}</pre>
    <CodeCaption>
      Next reads the options off <code>fetch</code> itself: this response is cached across requests and
      tagged, so a mutation can invalidate exactly this data later.
    </CodeCaption>
  </Snippet>

  <p>
    This is why the audit's blanket <code>export const dynamic = "force-dynamic"</code> plus
    <code>no-store</code> everywhere (<a href='17-applied-to-tracker.html#finding-7'>§17</a>) is so
    costly: it tells this extended <code>fetch</code> to skip the Data Cache on every call, throwing away
    a layer the framework hands you for free. The habit to build is the inverse: cache by default with
    a declared <code>revalidate</code> and
    <code>tags</code>, and reach for <code>no-store</code> only where the data is genuinely per-request.
    When you call through the database or an SDK instead of <code>fetch</code>, you don't get this
    automatically, which is exactly why <a href='#server'>§14.2</a>'s
    <code>cache()</code> and <code>"use cache"</code> exist to fill the gap.
  </p>

  <h2 id='swr-depth'>14.6 SWR, in more depth</h2>
  <p>
    On the client, the whole model rests on one idea:
    <strong>the key is the identity of the data.</strong> Two components that pass the same key read the
    same cache entry; a component that passes <code>null</code> as its key makes no request at all. Everything
    else (deduplication, revalidation, mutation) hangs off that key.
  </p>

  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-good'
      is:raw><span class="filename">hooks/useJobStatus.ts</span><span class="tok-dir">"use client"</span>;
<span class="tok-kw">import</span> useSWR <span class="tok-kw">from</span> <span class="tok-str">"swr"</span>;

<span class="tok-kw">export function</span> <span class="tok-fn">useJobStatus</span>(jobId: <span class="tok-fn">string</span> | <span class="tok-kw">null</span>) {
  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> <span class="tok-fn">useSWR</span>(
    <span class="tok-com">// A null key disables the request until we actually have an id.</span>
    jobId ? <span class="tok-str">`jobs/${jobId}`</span> : <span class="tok-kw">null</span>,
    fetcher,
    {
      <span class="tok-com">// Poll while the job runs; the last value stays visible between polls.</span>
      refreshInterval: 3000,
      <span class="tok-com">// Collapse duplicate requests for this key inside a 2s window.</span>
      dedupingInterval: 2000,
    },
  );
}</pre>
  </Snippet>

  <p>
    The revalidation triggers are the reason SWR feels alive without any manual wiring: by default
    it refetches on window focus and on network reconnect, plus on whatever
    <code>refreshInterval</code> you set. Crucially,
    <strong>it never blanks the existing content to do so.</strong> The stale value stays on screen while
    the new one loads, and swaps in when it arrives. That is the stale-while-revalidate contract, and
    it is the client-side echo of the loading doctrine in <a
      href='10-suspense-streaming.html#refetch'>§10</a
    >: revalidation is shown alongside content, never in place of it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Writes go through <code>mutate</code>, which is the client mirror of
    <code>revalidateTag</code>: instead of calling a parent back to refetch, you tell the cache that
    a key is stale and every reader of that key updates itself. Passing new data to <code
      >mutate</code
    > paints it optimistically and revalidates in the background, which pairs naturally with
    <a href='18-new-primitives.html#use-optimistic'>§18's <code>useOptimistic</code></a> for action-driven
    writes.
  </p>

  <h3 id='swr-vs-query'>Why SWR and not TanStack Query</h3>
  <p>
    The honest comparison first, because TanStack Query is an excellent library and the more popular
    one (roughly 12 million weekly downloads to SWR's 8 as of this writing, and growing faster). It
    is also the more <em>powerful</em> one: a dedicated
    <code>useMutation</code> hook with built-in optimistic updates and rollback, query invalidation primitives,
    infinite-query and pagination helpers, and first-class devtools. If we were building a client-heavy
    app where the browser cache <em>is</em>
    the data layer, TanStack Query would be the stronger tool, and general-audience comparisons rightly
    recommend it as the default for that world.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>We are not building that app</strong>, and that is the whole argument.
    <Chip k='next' />
    <Chip k='very' /> In this architecture the client cache's job is deliberately small: the server layer
    owns first-paint data, mutations flow through server actions with <code>revalidateTag</code>
    (<a href='05-client-server-boundaries.html#server-actions'>§5.8</a>), and optimism is
    <code>useOptimistic</code>. What remains for a client library (<a href='#when-client'>§14.7</a
    >'s interaction-born, on-demand, and polling cases) is exactly the surface SWR covers, in about
    4&nbsp;KB gzipped against TanStack Query's ~13&nbsp;KB, which matters under <a
      href='07-bundle-size-code-splitting.html'>§7</a
    >'s budget thinking. TanStack Query's extra machinery would mostly duplicate responsibilities
    the server layer already owns.
  </p>
  <p>
    The tiebreaker is alignment.
    <a href='https://swr.vercel.app' target='_blank' rel='noopener'>SWR</a> is built and maintained by
    Vercel, the same team that ships Next.js, and was designed from the start for use with Next: its
    defaults are designed against the App Router's behavior, its releases track Next's, and the
    Next.js docs use it in their own client-fetching examples. When
    the framework and the cache library share an owner, the seams between them are somebody's job to keep
    smooth. Choosing
    <a href='https://tanstack.com/query/latest' target='_blank' rel='noopener'>TanStack Query</a> here
    would buy capabilities this architecture works to make unnecessary, at three times the bundle weight
    and with one more vendor's opinions in the mix.
  </p>

  <h2 id='when-client'>14.7 When client fetching is the right tool</h2>
  <p>
    Server-first does not mean the client never fetches. It means client fetching is a
    <em>deliberate</em> choice for the cases that genuinely call for it, not the default for everything.
    <Chip k='react' />
    <Chip k='very' /> Those cases have a clear signature: the data is <strong
      >not needed at first paint</strong
    >, and it is
    <strong>born from an interaction, a poll, or rapidly-changing client state.</strong>
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Data that only exists after an interaction:</strong> search-as-you-type results, autocomplete
      suggestions, a filter applied in the browser. There is nothing to render on the server because the
      input hasn't happened yet.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Content inside a dialog, drawer, or sheet</strong> that isn't part of the main page and
      is opened on demand. It isn't in the first paint, so loading it when the panel opens is correct,
      not a smell.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Live or polling data:</strong> a job status, a notifications badge, anything that changes
      while the user watches it.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The dialog case is the one worth showing, because it is the clean inverse of the God component:
    the panel and its data are both loaded only when needed.
  </p>

  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-good'
      is:raw><span class="filename">components/programs/RowActions.tsx</span><span class="tok-dir">"use client"</span>;
<span class="tok-kw">import</span> { useState } <span class="tok-kw">from</span> <span class="tok-str">"react"</span>;
<span class="tok-kw">import</span> dynamic <span class="tok-kw">from</span> <span class="tok-str">"next/dynamic"</span>;

<span class="tok-com">// The drawer and its data are both born from the interaction: loaded on open.</span>
<span class="tok-kw">const</span> <span class="tok-fn">DetailsDrawer</span> = <span class="tok-fn">dynamic</span>(() =&gt;
  <span class="tok-kw">import</span>(<span class="tok-str">"./DetailsDrawer"</span>).<span class="tok-fn">then</span>((m) =&gt; m.DetailsDrawer)
);

<span class="tok-kw">export function</span> <span class="tok-fn">RowActions</span>({ id }: { id: <span class="tok-fn">string</span> }) {
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> [open, setOpen] = <span class="tok-fn">useState</span>(<span class="tok-kw">false</span>);
  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> (
    &lt;&gt;
      &lt;<span class="tok-fn">Button</span> <span class="tok-prop">onClick</span>={() =&gt; <span class="tok-fn">setOpen</span>(<span class="tok-kw">true</span>)}&gt;Details&lt;/<span class="tok-fn">Button</span>&gt;
      <span class="tok-com">{/* DetailsDrawer calls useDetails(id), a shared SWR hook, once mounted. */}</span>
      {open &amp;&amp; &lt;<span class="tok-fn">DetailsDrawer</span> <span class="tok-prop">id</span>={id} <span class="tok-prop">onClose</span>={() =&gt; <span class="tok-fn">setOpen</span>(<span class="tok-kw">false</span>)} /&gt;}
    &lt;/&gt;
  );
}</pre>
  </Snippet>

  <p>And the drawer itself, stubbed to the shape that matters:</p>

  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-good'
      is:raw><span class="filename">components/programs/DetailsDrawer.tsx - born from the click, fetches for itself</span><span class="tok-dir">"use client"</span>;
<span class="tok-kw">import</span> { useDetails } <span class="tok-kw">from</span> <span class="tok-str">"@/hooks/useDetails"</span>;

<span class="tok-kw">export function</span> <span class="tok-fn">DetailsDrawer</span>({ id, onClose }: { id: <span class="tok-fn">string</span>; onClose: () =&gt; <span class="tok-kw">void</span> }) {
  <span class="tok-com">// The shared SWR hook (§14.3): useSWR(`programs/${id}/details`, fetcher).</span>
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> { data, error, isLoading } = <span class="tok-fn">useDetails</span>(id);
  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> (
    &lt;<span class="tok-fn">Drawer</span> <span class="tok-prop">onClose</span>={onClose}&gt;
      &lt;<span class="tok-fn">DrawerTitle</span>&gt;Program details&lt;/<span class="tok-fn">DrawerTitle</span>&gt;
      {isLoading &amp;&amp; &lt;<span class="tok-fn">DetailsSkeleton</span> /&gt;}
      {error &amp;&amp; &lt;<span class="tok-fn">DrawerError</span> <span class="tok-prop">message</span>=<span class="tok-str">"Couldn't load the details."</span> /&gt;}
      {data &amp;&amp; &lt;<span class="tok-fn">DetailsBody</span> <span class="tok-prop">details</span>={data} /&gt;}
    &lt;/<span class="tok-fn">Drawer</span>&gt;
  );
}</pre>
    <CodeCaption>
      The component mounts only after the click, so the SWR request starts at the exact moment the
      user asks for the content: nothing about it touches the first paint. Loading, error, and data
      states all live inside the panel, and because the hook is the shared one from
      <a href='#client'>§14.3</a>, a second open (or any other component asking the same question)
      hits the cache instead of the network. This drawer showing its own skeleton is fine by
      <a href='10-suspense-streaming.html'>§10</a>'s rules: the panel is the container, and the
      content was never known.
    </CodeCaption>
  </Snippet>

  <p>
    The test is the one from
    <a href='08-server-resolvable-state.html#client-state'>§8.5</a>, applied to data: if the content
    is part of what the page <em>is</em>, the server should fetch it; if it is something the page <em
      >does</em
    > in response to the user, a client hook is the right home. The failure mode the audit found is the
    first kind masquerading as the second: a whole page's primary data loaded client-side as if it were
    interaction-born, when it was the page all along.
  </p>

  <h2 id='rules'>The rules</h2>
  <table class='tbl'>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Rule</th>
        <th>Scope</th>
        <th>Importance</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>
          All server data access lives in
          <strong>a dedicated data layer, wrapped in <code>cache()</code></strong>, marked <code
            >server-only</code
          >.
        </td>
        <td><Chip k='next' /></td>
        <td><Chip k='critical' /></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          Shared, non-personalized data is
          <strong>cached cross-request with <code>"use cache"</code></strong> and tagged for invalidation.
        </td>
        <td><Chip k='next' /></td>
        <td><Chip k='very' /></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          Client fetching goes through
          <strong>shared SWR/React Query hooks with canonical keys</strong>; never raw fetch in
          components or effects.
        </td>
        <td><Chip k='react' /></td>
        <td><Chip k='critical' /></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          Mutations <strong>invalidate rather than orchestrate</strong>: tags and
          <code>mutate()</code>, not callbacks and refetchAll.
        </td>
        <td><Chip k='next' /></td>
        <td><Chip k='critical' /></td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

